     5	## 1. The four questions in the book
     6	
     7	| Book question | What is settled / representative frontier results | What remains open |
     8	|---|---|---|
     9	| Better finite binary codes | Stabilizer/CSS constructions, graph/CWS codes, LP and shadow bounds are established. Panteleev–Kalachev [P1] settled the existence of asymptotically good qLDPC families: both rate and relative distance stay positive. This major post-2009 theorem does not determine individual small-code parameters. The 2025 SDP hierarchy [P2] is complete at all levels, not necessarily tractable at the level needed. The 2026 rational-certificate paper [P3] strengthens 18 finite-code upper bounds for lengths 6–19 and removes floating-point uncertainty from those certificates. | Exact finite maxima and many achievable trade-offs remain undetermined. A complete hierarchy does not mean its first practical relaxation settles every entry. |
    10	| Good decoding algorithms | Dinur–Hsieh–Lin–Vidick [P4] construct good qLDPC codes with linear-time decoders. Sparse Blossom [P5] reports, at 0.1% circuit-level depolarizing noise, processing both bases of distance-17 surface-code syndrome data in under one microsecond per round on a single core. A September 2026 preprint [P6] claims explicit capacity-approaching qLDPC **list** decoding in near-linear time. | There is no single noise-model-independent “best decoder.” Finite-length latency, circuit noise, measurement errors, degeneracy, accuracy, hardware connectivity, and unique versus list decoding must be matched. We did not benchmark or reproduce these decoders. [P6] is a preprint result, not a claim that arbitrary physical errors can be uniquely corrected near the Singleton radius. |
    11	| Nonbinary quantum codes | Ketkar et al. [P7] establish the finite-field stabilizer framework and multiple code families. Hermitian self-orthogonal generalized Reed–Solomon and related constructions continue to extend q-ary quantum MDS parameter families; He et al. [P8] give a 2025 example for specified lengths. Grassl maintains separate physical-q tables [T]. | The existence spectrum is not classified, even at small q. The ternary entries below retain distance gaps. Particular MDS families do not settle every q or length. |
    12	| Nonadditive codes | Cross et al. [P9] develop CWS codes; Yu–Chen–Oh [P10] give two infinite one-error-correcting families with code-space dimension 50% above the optimal stabilizer dimension at their parameters. Chuang et al. [P11] prove that every dimension-three CWS code extends to a dimension-four stabilizer code, excluding the seven-qubit CWS instance. General-code rational SDP bounds [P3] apply beyond stabilizers. | The unrestricted `((7,3,3))_2` question remains explicitly unresolved in [P12] and retains a 2–3 gap in the 2026 primary table [P3]. The same table leaves `((11,5,4))_2` and several other existence questions unresolved. |
