    54	
    55	## 1. Exact LP barrier and upper-bound certificate (C2–C3)
    56	
    57	Let P be a rank-K projector on n qubits. Use the standard Shor–Laflamme enumerators
    58	
    59	`A_j = sum_{wt(E)=j} |Tr(PE)|^2`,
    60	
    61	`B_j = sum_{wt(E)=j} Tr(PEPE)`
    62	
    63	for Hermitian Pauli strings E. Put `a_j=A_j/K^2`, `b_j=B_j/K`. The established MacWilliams identity and shadow inequalities [P3, Eqs. (5)–(11)] give
    64	
    65	`b_j = (K/2^n) sum_i K_j(i;n) a_i`,
    66	
    67	`s_j = (1/2^n) sum_i (-1)^i K_j(i;n) a_i >= 0`,
    68	
    69	where `K_j(i;n)` is the coefficient of `x^(n-j)y^j` in `(x+3y)^(n-i)(x-y)^i`.
    70	
    71	Necessary conditions at distance at least d are:
    72	
    73	- `a_0=1`, all `a_j>=0`;
    74	- `b_j=a_j` for `0<=j<d`;
    75	- `b_j>=a_j` for all remaining j;
    76	- every `s_j>=0`.
    77	
    78	These conditions permit degenerate codes. We do not add purity, integrality, CWS, or stabilizer restrictions to the general-code LP.
    79	
    80	### C2: a rational feasible point at K=3
    81	
    82	For `n=7, K=3, d=3`, take
    83	
    84	```text
    85	a = (1, 0, 0, 0, 7, 14, 56/3, 2)
    86	b = (1, 0, 0, 28, 63, 126, 112, 54)
    87	s = (1/12, 0, 7/4, 28/3, 49/4, 56, 343/12, 20).
    88	```
    89	
    90	Every listed condition follows by direct rational substitution. `results/lp-feasible-7-3-3.json` stores a. The independent verifier obtains the transforms by repeated polynomial multiplication, not by importing the generator's binomial Krawtchouk formula.
    91	
    92	This settles the proposed **LP-only** route negatively: no correct infeasibility certificate for these exact constraints exists at this target. It does **not** settle the complete SDP hierarchy, stronger relaxations, or existence of a physical projector realizing this enumerator.
    93	
    94	### C3: explicit contradiction at K=4
    95	
    96	For n=7 define the integer linear forms
    97	
    98	`F_j = 4 sum_i K_j(i;7) a_i - 128 a_j`,
    99	
   100	`T_j = sum_i (-1)^i K_j(i;7) a_i`.
   101	
   102	A distance-three code with K=4 requires `F_0=F_1=F_2=0` and `T_0,T_1>=0`. Coefficient-by-coefficient polynomial expansion gives the **identical zero linear form**
   103	
   104	```text
   105	0 = a_0 + 4 a_1 + a_2 + 2 a_3
   106	    + (T_0 + T_1)/16
   107	    - 5 F_0/128 - F_1/64 - F_2/128.
   108	```
   109	
   110	Substitution of the necessary constraints makes the right-hand side at least 1, a contradiction. All nonnegative multipliers and equality multipliers are in `results/lp-infeasible-7-4-3.json`. The verifier checks every coefficient is exactly zero and the normalized contradiction constant is exactly one.
   111	
   112	If a code of dimension greater than four existed, any four-dimensional subspace would inherit every Knill–Laflamme error-detection identity. Thus C3 excludes all `K>=4`, not only exactly K=4. Together with C1 it certifies the known interval `2 <= K_max(7,3) <= 3`.
   113	
   114	## 2. A stabilizer-parent obstruction beyond CWS (C4)
   115	
   116	**Lemma.** Let V be a dimension-D qubit stabilizer code with D at least two, and Q a rank-r subspace of V with `2<=r<=D` and `r>D/2`. If Q detects every Pauli of weight below d, then V also detects every such Pauli. Equivalently, taking a subcode larger than half the parent cannot improve its distance.
   117	
   118	**Proof.** Fix a Hermitian Pauli E of weight below d and let S be V's stabilizer group, with signs chosen for its common +1 eigenspace.
   119	
   120	1. If E anticommutes with an element of S, E sends V into its orthogonal complement. Consequently its compression to V is zero: it is already detected by V.
   121	2. If E acts as a signed stabilizer, its restriction to V is scalar: again V detects it.
   122	3. In the remaining case E commutes with S but is not a signed element of S. It preserves V. Its restricted square is the identity. Moreover
   123	   `Tr_V(E) = (1/|S|) sum_{g in S} Tr(Eg) = 0`,
   124	   because no `Eg` is a scalar identity. Its +1 and -1 eigenspaces V+ and V- therefore each have dimension D/2.
   125	4. The elementary intersection bound gives
   126	   `dim(Q intersect V+) >= r + D/2 - D > 0`,
   127	   and the same for V-. Choose nonzero vectors in both intersections. If the Q-compression were `c I_Q`, its expectation on the first vector forces c=1; its expectation on the second forces c=-1. This contradicts detection on Q.
   128	
   129	Case 3 is impossible. Every low-weight E is therefore in case 1 or 2, which is exactly detection by V. QED.
   130	
   131	An independent viewpoint is the Hermitian higher-rank numerical range [P13]: a rank-r scalar compression of a Hermitian matrix must lie between its r-th smallest and r-th largest eigenvalues. For equal ±1 multiplicities and `r>D/2`, that interval is empty. The strict inequality matters: at `r=D/2`, the span of equal superpositions of paired positive/negative eigenvectors has zero compression.
   132	
   133	**Seven-qubit corollary.** Five independent commuting Pauli operators acting scalarly on a hypothetical rank-three code define a dimension-four stabilizer parent. (Six independent generators would already leave dimension two.) C4 would make that parent a `((7,4,3))` code, contradicting C3. Hence any hypothetical `((7,3,3))` has **at most four independent common Pauli stabilizers**. General rank-three subspaces can have zero such stabilizers; nothing here excludes them. Local unitary changes of basis also preserve the support-size error-detection condition, but this observation is not a classification of all codes.
   134	
   135	## 3. Known CWS barrier and its independent finite control (C5)
   136	
   137	A standard-form CWS code has orthonormal basis `Z^c |G>` for a graph state G and binary words c. Translate all words by one of them, a local Pauli operation, to assume the set contains 0. Write a Pauli up to phase as `X^x Z^z`, with induced word `e=z+Gamma*x` over F2.
   138	
   139	- For e nonzero, error detection requires that e not be a difference of two distinct code words.
   140	- For e zero, the Pauli acts diagonally in the graph basis. Its phases must agree on every selected word; with 0 present this requires `c dot x=0` for every c.
   141	
   142	For three words `{0,a,b}`, distinctness makes a,b linearly independent. Their nonzero differences are exactly `{a,b,a+b}`. Adjoining `a+b` adds no new difference, and its zero-syndrome phase constraints follow by linearity. Thus the four-word linear code `{0,a,b,a+b}` has the same error-detection property and is a stabilizer code. For n=7,d=3 this contradicts C3. This is the known structure theorem of [P11], not a new result.
   143	
   144	As a redundant exact control, `verify.py` enumerates all 1,044 seven-vertex graph representatives in NetworkX's graph atlas. For each frame it constructs every induced error of weights one and two, imposes every zero-syndrome phase constraint, and checks all pairs of nonzero admissible words. **Zero** frames admit a three-word set. **864** of the graph frames have a graph-state stabilizer of weight below three, so simply ignoring degeneracy would not have checked the same search space. The atlas is a shared external data dependency; the analytic argument above removes it from the universal theorem's proof.
   145	
   146	## 4. Actual open-instance attack: eleven-qubit symmetric graph frames (C6–C7)
   147	
   148	### Finite contract and exhaustive reduction
   149	
   150	The full target is `((11,5,4))_2`, which would beat the dimension-four lower bound in [P3, Table 1]. We restricted the **construction frame**, not the classical word set, to the 32 simple undirected circulant graphs
   151	
   152	`vertices = Z/11Z`,
   153	
   154	`edges i~j iff j-i belongs to +/-D`,
   155	
   156	`D is any subset of {1,2,3,4,5}`.
   157	
   158	For each graph, generate every Pauli of weights one, two, and three: `33+495+4455=4983` errors. Translate a prospective word set to contain 0. Define V to be every nonzero word that is neither an induced nonzero error nor forbidden by a zero-syndrome phase constraint. Two vertices a,b in V are adjacent exactly when their difference is not an induced nonzero error. The phase constraints are linear, so the difference automatically satisfies them.
   159	
   160	A five-dimensional code exists in this frame **if and only if** this graph on V has a clique of size four. A larger code would contain a five-word subcode, so excluding size five excludes all larger dimensions in the same frame.
   161	
   162	The search program exhaustively branches on increasing vertex indices, intersecting with adjacency at each step; the population-count cutoff only prunes branches with fewer vertices than still needed. It considers every prospective four-clique. The independent verifier reconstructs all error syndromes using set symmetric differences, rebuilds compatibility edges, and uses a separate maximal-clique enumeration. Neither imports the other's implementation.
   163	
   164	### Observed exact result and mathematical consequence
   165	
   166	- All 32 masks were checked, with no omitted connection set.
   167	- Twelve frames have no admissible nonzero word.
   168	- Twenty frames have 165 admissible nonzero words and compatibility-clique maximum exactly three.
   169	- Thus no frame has K=5; twenty frames have K=4.
   170	- `results/circulant-11-5-4.json` records all raw frame inputs, admissible words, and positive controls.
   171	- `results/circulant-verification.json` records the independently reconstructed result.
   172	
   173	This proves the maximum is exactly four **within this family**.
   174	
   175	**Prior-art resolution:** [P14, §III.C] already reports the same upper bound for the entire eleven-qubit CWS class, after exhaustive LC-class searches. Its n=11 result must not be confused with the heuristic clique search explicitly used at n=12. The present computation therefore reproduces a strict subfamily of a known result. It supplies a small independently checked artifact, not a stronger theorem.
   176	
   177	**Vertex-transitive extension.** The automorphism group of a vertex-transitive graph on 11 vertices has order divisible by 11 by orbit–stabilizer. By Cauchy's theorem it has an element of order 11. As a permutation of 11 vertices this element is an 11-cycle. Relabeling along that cycle presents the graph as one of the 32 circulant graphs above. Consequently the same bound covers every **vertex-transitive graph-state frame** on 11 vertices. It does not require that the selected codeword set itself be cyclic or transitive. It does not cover an arbitrary non-vertex-transitive frame, much less every quantum code.
   178	
   179	### Explicit positive control and independent distance checks
   180	
   181	The first positive frame has offsets D={1,2}. Its four word labels are
   182	
   183	`{0, 189, 599, 746}`,
   184	
   185	where qubit j is integer bit j. An explicit amplitude formula is
   186	
   187	`|c> = 2^(-11/2) sum_s (-1)^(sum_{edges ij} s_i*s_j + sum_j c_j*s_j) |s>`.
   188	
   189	Edges and all conventions are in `results/circulant-11-4-4-anchor.json`. The CWS error criterion verifies distance at least four. A second implementation builds all 2,048 integer amplitudes for each of the four vectors and checks all 4,984 Pauli compressions of weights zero through three, without floating arithmetic. The Gram matrix is `2048 I_4` before normalization. The weight-four string `XYXIIYIIIII` has a nonscalar compression, proving distance exactly four. The implementation represents Y by XZ, differing only by a global phase; scalar-versus-nonscalar and zero-versus-nonzero compression are invariant under that phase.
   190	
   191	This code is a **positive control**, not a new parameter record. The unrestricted primary interval remains `4 <= K_max(11,4) <= 7`.
   192	
