RESULTS.md:17:| C7 | A particular `((11,4,4))_2` positive-control code is explicitly given and has distance exactly 4. | **Certification** | Reaches, but does not beat, the known dimension-four lower bound. Independent CWS and integer state-vector checks passed. |
RESULTS.md:18:| D1 | The considered open targets have not been demonstrated to be “not too hard.” The tested cheap construction route is exhausted. | **Empirical evidence** for the screening history plus C2/C4/C6's exact restrictions | Negative selection decision. It does not assert that the unrestricted problems are impossible or objectively intractable. |
RESULTS.md:37:FULL INSTANCE: OPEN. No general rank-3 projector or nonexistence certificate is claimed.
RESULTS.md:47:/Users/jinleic/jinleic-workspace/scratch/ai4s-venv/bin/python code/build_lp_certificates.py
RESULTS.md:55:## 1. Exact LP barrier and upper-bound certificate (C2–C3)
RESULTS.md:92:This settles the proposed **LP-only** route negatively: no correct infeasibility certificate for these exact constraints exists at this target. It does **not** settle the complete SDP hierarchy, stronger relaxations, or existence of a physical projector realizing this enumerator.
RESULTS.md:112:If a code of dimension greater than four existed, any four-dimensional subspace would inherit every Knill–Laflamme error-detection identity. Thus C3 excludes all `K>=4`, not only exactly K=4. Together with C1 it certifies the known interval `2 <= K_max(7,3) <= 3`.
RESULTS.md:120:1. If E anticommutes with an element of S, E sends V into its orthogonal complement. Consequently its compression to V is zero: it is already detected by V.
RESULTS.md:133:**Seven-qubit corollary.** Five independent commuting Pauli operators acting scalarly on a hypothetical rank-three code define a dimension-four stabilizer parent. (Six independent generators would already leave dimension two.) C4 would make that parent a `((7,4,3))` code, contradicting C3. Hence any hypothetical `((7,3,3))` has **at most four independent common Pauli stabilizers**. General rank-three subspaces can have zero such stabilizers; nothing here excludes them. Local unitary changes of basis also preserve the support-size error-detection condition, but this observation is not a classification of all codes.
RESULTS.md:142:For three words `{0,a,b}`, distinctness makes a,b linearly independent. Their nonzero differences are exactly `{a,b,a+b}`. Adjoining `a+b` adds no new difference, and its zero-syndrome phase constraints follow by linearity. Thus the four-word linear code `{0,a,b,a+b}` has the same error-detection property and is a stabilizer code. For n=7,d=3 this contradicts C3. This is the known structure theorem of [P11], not a new result.
RESULTS.md:146:## 4. Actual open-instance attack: eleven-qubit symmetric graph frames (C6–C7)
RESULTS.md:148:### Finite contract and exhaustive reduction
RESULTS.md:162:The search program exhaustively branches on increasing vertex indices, intersecting with adjacency at each step; the population-count cutoff only prunes branches with fewer vertices than still needed. It considers every prospective four-clique. The independent verifier reconstructs all error syndromes using set symmetric differences, rebuilds compatibility edges, and uses a separate maximal-clique enumeration. Neither imports the other's implementation.
RESULTS.md:175:**Prior-art resolution:** [P14, §III.C] already reports the same upper bound for the entire eleven-qubit CWS class, after exhaustive LC-class searches. Its n=11 result must not be confused with the heuristic clique search explicitly used at n=12. The present computation therefore reproduces a strict subfamily of a known result. It supplies a small independently checked artifact, not a stronger theorem.
RESULTS.md:177:**Vertex-transitive extension.** The automorphism group of a vertex-transitive graph on 11 vertices has order divisible by 11 by orbit–stabilizer. By Cauchy's theorem it has an element of order 11. As a permutation of 11 vertices this element is an 11-cycle. Relabeling along that cycle presents the graph as one of the 32 circulant graphs above. Consequently the same bound covers every **vertex-transitive graph-state frame** on 11 vertices. It does not require that the selected codeword set itself be cyclic or transitive. It does not cover an arbitrary non-vertex-transitive frame, much less every quantum code.
RESULTS.md:191:This code is a **positive control**, not a new parameter record. The unrestricted primary interval remains `4 <= K_max(11,4) <= 7`.
RESULTS.md:195:The user asked for still-open but not-too-hard problems. This project has **not established that selection criterion**. The seven-qubit route starts behind a 2009 structural obstruction; its remaining feasible LP data lack projector realizability. The eleven-qubit assay was aimed at a genuine unrestricted gap, but the stronger 2019 CWS classification was initially missed. Both the locally tested family and the larger CWS class are already closed. The existence of simulators or fast certificate checkers is not an argument that the remaining problem is easy.
RESULTS.md:197:**Single most valuable next action:** derive a bounded realizability constraint for **genuinely non-CWS rank-five projectors** at `((11,5,4))_2`, beyond the weight-enumerator relaxation. No such missing lemma or promising unrestricted ansatz is established here. The earlier idea of searching non-vertex-transitive CWS frames is **withdrawn** in light of [P14]. Further CWS search and unstructured numerical restarts are not justified. This is an exact representation blocker and a negative selection decision, not a promise of a solution.
RESULTS.md:199:## 6. Independent review
RESULTS.md:201:The first hostile review ran through Codex CLI 0.162.1 using the configured **gpt-6.1-sol**, **max** effort, **read-only** sandbox; it exited 0. Prompt, report, logs and input hashes are in `review/codex-prompt.md`, `review/codex-review.md`, `review/codex-stderr.log`, and `review/invocation.json`.
RESULTS.md:203:- **Referee verdict:** READY as a restricted-certificate and negative-selection report; **zero findings**.
RESULTS.md:204:- **Disposition:** accepted the absence of mathematical objections, not an assertion of literature completeness. The reviewer independently recomputed the rational LP, Steane distance, all 32 eleven-qubit compatibility graphs and clique obstructions, and selected integer-amplitude controls; it source-audited the full eleven-qubit compression loop and graph-atlas loop. Exact details of what it did are in the unedited report.
RESULTS.md:205:- **Author-discovered prior-art issue, found while that frozen review ran:** [P14] gives a stronger all-CWS exclusion at n=11,d=4. Verified directly in its primary text (§III.C), then pinned the PDF. **Disposition: fixed.** C6 is now labeled Reproduction, the novelty-unclear description is removed, and further non-vertex-transitive CWS search is explicitly withdrawn. The original referee report is retained verbatim as a review of the earlier hashed snapshot; its READY verdict is not represented as review of this later literature correction.
RESULTS.md:206:- **Target-selection issue:** neither original nor amended assay established an easy open target. **Disposition: retained as a failure to meet that criterion**, prominently in README.md and STATUS.md. No newly solved open instance is claimed.
STATUS.md:3:Snapshot: **2026-10-14, America/Los_Angeles**. This is a focused public-source review, not an exhaustive priority search. Primary sources support every substantive status statement below. A table gap is reported as a **table gap**, not proof that no recent paper has resolved it. Preprint claims and locally replayed certificates are distinct.
STATUS.md:7:| Book question | What is settled / representative frontier results | What remains open |
STATUS.md:9:| Better finite binary codes | Stabilizer/CSS constructions, graph/CWS codes, LP and shadow bounds are established. Panteleev–Kalachev [P1] settled the existence of asymptotically good qLDPC families: both rate and relative distance stay positive. This major post-2009 theorem does not determine individual small-code parameters. The 2025 SDP hierarchy [P2] is complete at all levels, not necessarily tractable at the level needed. The 2026 rational-certificate paper [P3] strengthens 18 finite-code upper bounds for lengths 6–19 and removes floating-point uncertainty from those certificates. | Exact finite maxima and many achievable trade-offs remain undetermined. A complete hierarchy does not mean its first practical relaxation settles every entry. |
STATUS.md:10:| Good decoding algorithms | Dinur–Hsieh–Lin–Vidick [P4] construct good qLDPC codes with linear-time decoders. Sparse Blossom [P5] reports, at 0.1% circuit-level depolarizing noise, processing both bases of distance-17 surface-code syndrome data in under one microsecond per round on a single core. A September 2026 preprint [P6] claims explicit capacity-approaching qLDPC **list** decoding in near-linear time. | There is no single noise-model-independent “best decoder.” Finite-length latency, circuit noise, measurement errors, degeneracy, accuracy, hardware connectivity, and unique versus list decoding must be matched. We did not benchmark or reproduce these decoders. [P6] is a preprint result, not a claim that arbitrary physical errors can be uniquely corrected near the Singleton radius. |
STATUS.md:14:**Stronger prior art found during the final check:** Rigby–Olivier–Jarvis [P14, §III.C] report an exhaustive search over LC-inequivalent graph states for n=11,d=4: 3,060 graph classes yield K=4 and none yields a larger code. Thus the eleven-qubit target is already excluded throughout **CWS**, including non-vertex-transitive frames. Our 32-frame result is only a restricted reproduction of that stronger published exclusion. This source also reports CWS exclusions for K=25,26 at n=7,d=2 (§III.A) and K=13 at n=9,d=3 (§III.B). These statements do not close the unrestricted general-code gaps.
STATUS.md:28:| 7, 3 | 2–3 | `((7,3,3))_2` | Initial target; known CWS obstruction and numerical history make “not too hard” unjustified. Exact barriers reproduced, not an open-problem solution. |
STATUS.md:29:| 7, 2 | 24–26 | `((7,25,2))_2` | Screened; [P14, §III.A] already excludes K=25,26 within CWS. No local computation claimed. |
STATUS.md:30:| 9, 3 | 12–13 | `((9,13,3))_2` | Screened; [P14, §III.B] already excludes K=13 within CWS. No local computation claimed. |
STATUS.md:31:| 11, 4 | 4–7 | `((11,5,4))_2` | A 32-frame exact assay was performed, but final primary-source checking found that [P14, §III.C] already excludes every CWS frame. Restricted reproduction only; global interval unchanged. |
STATUS.md:33:The 2026 source explicitly presents lower/upper gaps, rather than a statement inferred solely from an unsuccessful search. The earlier VarQEC primary paper [P12] explicitly describes numerical evidence against the seven-qubit code, not a proof. Current NumQI implementation documentation also calls it open, but this secondary/program-documentation source is not the basis of the status claim: <https://numqi.github.io/numqi/application/qecc/qecc733/>.
STATUS.md:37:Fetched individually on the snapshot date. These are candidate **table gaps only**; not all are approved open research targets.
STATUS.md:45:| 2 | `[[14,3,d]]_2` | 4–5 | <https://www.codetables.de/QECC/QECC.php?q=4&n=14&k=3>; **not selected as confidently open**, because of the subsequent primary preprint below. |
STATUS.md:49:**Important rejected candidate:** Mansehej Singh, *The Optimal Distance of Fourteen-Qubit Stabilizer Codes Encoding Three Qubits*, Zenodo preprint v1.0, 10 August 2026, DOI [10.5281/zenodo.21874338](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21874338), claims nonexistence of `[[14,3,5]]_2`, including degenerate codes, and a Lean formalization. Its primary metadata was accessible at <https://zenodo.org/api/records/21874337> (redirected to record 21874338); the HTML landing pages returned HTTP 403. This project did **not** replay or audit that formalization. It records a primary unreviewed-preprint claim, neither a locally certified resolution nor an unqualified open problem. An earlier August 5 note by Daniel Kirtchakov only proves automorphism restrictions and explicitly leaves existence open: <https://github.com/05oz/certify/blob/main/qec1435-paper/note.md>. These different scopes and dates must not be conflated.
STATUS.md:51:## 3. Target selection and difficulty verdict
STATUS.md:55:The eleven-qubit assay was intended to attack the book's finite-code construction question, but the initially missed 2019 classification means the chosen construction class was already closed. This is a baseline-screening failure, not open-problem progress. The defensible deliverable is a **negative tractability screening decision plus exact restricted reproductions**, not a claimed successful choice of an easy open physics problem. No further CWS search at these parameters is justified.
STATUS.md:60:- **[P2]** Gerard Anglès Munné, Andrew Nemec, and Felix Huber, *SDP bounds on quantum codes*, arXiv preprint, v2 (2025); [arXiv:2408.10323](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.10323). The practical exclusions named in this work receive exact certificates in [P3].
STATUS.md:61:- **[P3]** Gerard Anglès Munné and Felix Huber, *SDP bounds on quantum codes: rational certificates*, arXiv preprint (2026); [arXiv:2603.19901](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.19901), especially Eq. (11) and Table 1. **Nemec is not an author of this follow-up.** Pinned PDF: `source/rational-sdp-2603.19901.pdf`.
STATUS.md:66:- **[P8]** Xianmang He, Jingli Wang, Chunfang Huang, and Yindong Chen, *Exploring new lengths for q-ary quantum MDS codes with larger distance*, **PLOS ONE 20(6)**, e0325027 (2025); DOI [10.1371/journal.pone.0325027](https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0325027). This is a representative recent construction, not a universal optimality theorem.
