   146	## 4. Actual open-instance attack: eleven-qubit symmetric graph frames (C6–C7)
   147	
   148	### Finite contract and exhaustive reduction
   149	
   150	The full target is `((11,5,4))_2`, which would beat the dimension-four lower bound in [P3, Table 1]. We restricted the **construction frame**, not the classical word set, to the 32 simple undirected circulant graphs
   151	
   152	`vertices = Z/11Z`,
   153	
   154	`edges i~j iff j-i belongs to +/-D`,
   155	
   156	`D is any subset of {1,2,3,4,5}`.
   157	
   158	For each graph, generate every Pauli of weights one, two, and three: `33+495+4455=4983` errors. Translate a prospective word set to contain 0. Define V to be every nonzero word that is neither an induced nonzero error nor forbidden by a zero-syndrome phase constraint. Two vertices a,b in V are adjacent exactly when their difference is not an induced nonzero error. The phase constraints are linear, so the difference automatically satisfies them.
   159	
   160	A five-dimensional code exists in this frame **if and only if** this graph on V has a clique of size four. A larger code would contain a five-word subcode, so excluding size five excludes all larger dimensions in the same frame.
   161	
   162	The search program exhaustively branches on increasing vertex indices, intersecting with adjacency at each step; the population-count cutoff only prunes branches with fewer vertices than still needed. It considers every prospective four-clique. The independent verifier reconstructs all error syndromes using set symmetric differences, rebuilds compatibility edges, and uses a separate maximal-clique enumeration. Neither imports the other's implementation.
   163	
   164	### Observed exact result and mathematical consequence
   165	
   166	- All 32 masks were checked, with no omitted connection set.
   167	- Twelve frames have no admissible nonzero word.
   168	- Twenty frames have 165 admissible nonzero words and compatibility-clique maximum exactly three.
   169	- Thus no frame has K=5; twenty frames have K=4.
   170	- `results/circulant-11-5-4.json` records all raw frame inputs, admissible words, and positive controls.
   171	- `results/circulant-verification.json` records the independently reconstructed result.
   172	
   173	This proves the maximum is exactly four **within this family**.
   174	
   175	**Prior-art resolution:** [P14, §III.C] already reports the same upper bound for the entire eleven-qubit CWS class, after exhaustive LC-class searches. Its n=11 result must not be confused with the heuristic clique search explicitly used at n=12. The present computation therefore reproduces a strict subfamily of a known result. It supplies a small independently checked artifact, not a stronger theorem.
   176	
   177	**Vertex-transitive extension.** The automorphism group of a vertex-transitive graph on 11 vertices has order divisible by 11 by orbit–stabilizer. By Cauchy's theorem it has an element of order 11. As a permutation of 11 vertices this element is an 11-cycle. Relabeling along that cycle presents the graph as one of the 32 circulant graphs above. Consequently the same bound covers every **vertex-transitive graph-state frame** on 11 vertices. It does not require that the selected codeword set itself be cyclic or transitive. It does not cover an arbitrary non-vertex-transitive frame, much less every quantum code.
   193	## 5. Exact blocker and next-action decision
   194	
   195	The user asked for still-open but not-too-hard problems. This project has **not established that selection criterion**. The seven-qubit route starts behind a 2009 structural obstruction; its remaining feasible LP data lack projector realizability. The eleven-qubit assay was aimed at a genuine unrestricted gap, but the stronger 2019 CWS classification was initially missed. Both the locally tested family and the larger CWS class are already closed. The existence of simulators or fast certificate checkers is not an argument that the remaining problem is easy.
   196	
   197	**Single most valuable next action:** derive a bounded realizability constraint for **genuinely non-CWS rank-five projectors** at `((11,5,4))_2`, beyond the weight-enumerator relaxation. No such missing lemma or promising unrestricted ansatz is established here. The earlier idea of searching non-vertex-transitive CWS frames is **withdrawn** in light of [P14]. Further CWS search and unstructured numerical restarts are not justified. This is an exact representation blocker and a negative selection decision, not a promise of a solution.
